NAME
    overlay - a copy-on-write root: keep writes on another disk

SYNOPSIS
    overlay
    overlay on <disk> [--reset]
    overlay off
    overlay changes
    overlay merge
    overlay drop

DESCRIPTION
    With an overlay, `/` comes from two disks. Reads come from the second disk
    if it has the file and from the real root if it does not. Every write lands
    on the second disk, so the real root is not touched. Deleting a file records
    that it is gone, on the second disk too. <disk> is an address prefix or a
    label. Everything here is root's, and `on` and `off` take effect at the next
    boot.

    Three things come from this:

      Run from a read-only disk. A loot disk cannot hold a password. Boot from
      it with a hard drive in the machine and choose "Run from here, saving to
      the hard drive". You are asked at each boot, because a read-only disk has
      nowhere to remember the answer.

      An appliance that resets. `overlay on <disk> --reset` forgets everything
      at every boot: whatever a kiosk's visitors did is gone at the next one.

      Try something, then keep it or don't. `overlay on <disk>`, reboot, install
      or change what you like, and read `overlay changes`. Then `overlay merge`
      writes it all to the real root, or `overlay drop` forgets it.

SUBCOMMANDS
    overlay        What is overlaid, and how much has changed.
    on <disk>      From the next boot, writes land on <disk>. --reset forgets
                   them at every boot. Nothing else on that disk is touched: the
                   writes are in a directory of their own, /tos-overlay.
    off            From the next boot this is the real root. What was written
                   stays on the disk, and `overlay on` picks it up again.
    changes        Every file the overlay holds (+) or hides (-).
    merge          Write all of it to the real root. Reboot to finish. If it
                   stops part way, nothing is lost: run it again.
    drop           Forget all of it at the next boot.

SAFETY
    The second disk can replace any file on the system, the kernel included, so
    whoever can write to it can do anything. That is why it is no longer mounted
    once it holds the overlay, and why `mount` refuses it.

    If that disk is missing at boot, the machine runs on the real root and every
    write is permanent. `doctor` says so as an error. This is also the way back
    from an overlay that broke something: take the disk out and boot.

SEE ALSO
    jbod, pkg, kiosk, srm
